          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-measurable-rectangle": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-pi-system": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-lambda-system": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-continuity-from-below-for-measures": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-dynkin-pi-lambda": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tonelli-and-fubini-for-completed-product-measures": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-simple-functions-with-finite-measure-support-are-dense-in-l-p-for-finite-p": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure spaces,\nlet $\\overline{\\mu\\times\\nu}$ be their completed product measure, and put\n$H_X=L^2(X,\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ and $H_Y=L^2(Y,\\nu;\\mathbb C)$ with the\nsecond-variable-linear pairing fixed in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. There is a unique unitary\n\n$$T:H_X\\widehat\\otimes H_Y\\longrightarrow L^2(X\\times Y,\\overline{\\mu\\times\\nu};\\mathbb C)$$\n\nwhose value on each elementary tensor is\n$T(f\\otimes g)(x,y)=f(x)g(y)$ almost everywhere. In particular, the Hilbert\ntensor completion is identified with the full product $L^2$ space, not only\nwith the closure of a chosen class of product functions.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, §1.4 (orthogonal sums and tensor products), pp. 45–47",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 317-page book; §1.4, printed pp. 45–47."
            },
            {
              "title": "Jan Dereziński, Mathematical Introduction to Quantum Field Theory, lecture notes, §5.3 (tensor products), pp. 34–35",
              "url": "https://www.fuw.edu.pl/~derezins/qft-lectures.pdf",
              "locator": "Full 136-page author-hosted notes; PDF pp. 34–35."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Bosonic and fermionic Fock operators with exact domains",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement",
          "def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph",
          "def-densely-defined-closed-and-closable-operator",
          "def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator",
          "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-densely-defined-closed-and-closable-operator": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and let $H$ be a separable\ncomplex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ define the bosonic\ncreation and annihilation maps $a^\\dagger(h)$ and $a(h)$ and the fermionic maps\n$c^\\dagger(h)$ and $c(h)$ as in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Write $a_n(h)$ and $a_n^\\dagger(h)$ for\ntheir restrictions to the $n$th bosonic sector. For bosons set\n\n$$D(a(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\|a_n(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\},\\qquad D(a^\\dagger(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.$$\n\nThen $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)$ are Hilbert spaces,\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is dense, and it is a graph core for both maximal\nbosonic sector maps. The bosonic operators on their displayed domains are\nclosed and mutually adjoint, and on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ satisfy\n\n$$[a(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad [a(h),a(k)]=[a^\\dagger(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=0.$$\n\nThe fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the\nwhole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space\n\n$$\\{c(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad \\{c(h),c(k)\\}=\\{c^\\dagger(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=0.$$\n\nThis is a mathematical construction of the two Fock representations; it does\nnot assign particle statistics to a physical species.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jan Dereziński, Mathematical Introduction to Quantum Field Theory, lecture notes, §§5.4–5.5 and 5.8 (Fock sectors and creation/annihilation)",
              "url": "https://www.fuw.edu.pl/~derezins/qft-lectures.pdf",
              "locator": "Full 136-page author-hosted notes; PDF pp. 35–39."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §§2.2–2.4 (free-field modes, multi-particle states and Fock space)",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qft.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 155-page author-hosted lecture notes; printed pp. 23–25 and 29–30."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-fock-one-mode-splitting",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "One-mode factorization of symmetric Fock space",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement",
          "lem-orthogonal-complement-is-closed",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-orthogonal-complement-is-closed": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ be a complex\nHilbert space, let $e\\in H$ have norm one, and put\n$K=e^\\perp=\\{v\\in H:\\langle e,v\\rangle_2=0\\}$. For\n$\\psi_m\\in K_s^{(m)}$ and $k,m\\ge0$, write\n$|k\\rangle:=e^{\\otimes k}\\in(\\mathbb Ce)_s^{(k)}$ (with $|0\\rangle=1$),\nand define\n\n$$J_{k,m}(|k\\rangle\\otimes\\psi_m) =\\sqrt{\\binom{k+m}{k}}\\, P_{s,k+m}(e^{\\otimes k}\\otimes\\psi_m).$$\n\nThe map $J_{k,m}$ extends over the sector Hilbert tensor products and the\northogonal direct sums to a unitary\n\n$$J:\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb Ce)\\widehat\\otimes\\mathcal F_s(K) \\longrightarrow\\mathcal F_s(H).$$\n\nUnder this unitary, bosonic creation and annihilation by $e$ act on the first\nfactor as the one-mode ladder maps\n$|k\\rangle\\mapsto\\sqrt{k+1}|k+1\\rangle$ and\n$|k\\rangle\\mapsto\\sqrt{k}|k-1\\rangle$, respectively, tensored with the\nidentity on $\\mathcal F_s(K)$, on finite-particle vectors.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jan Dereziński, Mathematical Introduction to Quantum Field Theory, lecture notes, §5.9 (the exponential law for Fock spaces)",
              "url": "https://www.fuw.edu.pl/~derezins/qft-lectures.pdf",
              "locator": "Full 136-page author-hosted notes; §5.9, PDF pp. 39–40."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Second-quantized multipliers, joint spectral measure and exact domains",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator",
          "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint",
          "def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph",
          "def-densely-defined-closed-and-closable-operator",
          "def-projection-valued-measure",
          "def-complex-exponential",
          "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures",
